# Flutter: StopWatch

## Scenario

You are developing a stopwatch application in Flutter that displays the elapsed time since the app started running. The application has a State class with a Timer object and an integer variable to track elapsed seconds. When the user taps a "Start" button, the startTimer() function should be called to begin updating the elapsed time display every second.

The current implementation has a placeholder startTimer() function that needs to be completed. The function must initialize and start a periodic timer that increments the elapsed time counter by 1 second at regular intervals, allowing the UI to update and display the running time to the user.

## Question

Which of the following correctly completes the startTimer() function to create a timer that increments the elapsed time by 1 second at regular intervals?

A) timer = Timer.periodic(Duration(seconds: 1), (timer) { elapsedSeconds++; });

B) timer = Timer(Duration(seconds: 1), () { elapsedSeconds++; });

C) timer = setInterval(Duration(seconds: 1), () { elapsedSeconds++; });

D) timer = Timer.periodic(Duration(milliseconds: 1000), (timer) { elapsedSeconds += 1000; });

# Flutter: Increment Button

## Scenario

You are developing a Flutter application with a stateful widget that manages a counter. The widget displays the current count on the screen and includes an "Increment" button. When the button is pressed, the counter should increase by 1, and an alert dialog should appear showing the new count value.

Here is the code structure:

```dart
class CounterApp extends StatefulWidget {
  @override
  _CounterAppState createState() => _CounterAppState();
}

class _CounterAppState extends State<CounterApp> {
  int counter = 0;

  void _incrementCounter() {
    setState(() {
      counter++;
    });
    showDialog(
      context: context,
      builder: (context) => AlertDialog(
        title: Text('Counter'),
        content: Text('Count: $counter'),
      ),
    );
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      body: Center(
        child: Column(
          mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.center,
          children: [
            Text('Current Count: $counter'),
            SizedBox(height: 20),
            ElevatedButton(
              onPressed: _incrementCounter,
              child: Text('Increment'),
            ),
          ],
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

The user starts the application and immediately presses the Increment button once.

## Question

What is the output displayed on the screen and in the alert dialog box after pressing the Increment button?

## Options

A) Screen displays "Current Count: 0" and the alert box shows "Count: 0"

B) Screen displays "Current Count: 1" and the alert box shows "Count: 1"

C) Screen displays "Current Count: 1" and the alert box shows "Count: 0"

D) Screen displays "Current Count: 0" and the alert box shows "Count: 1"

# Flutter: Futures

## Scenario

You are working on a Flutter application that needs to handle asynchronous operations. Your team lead has written the following Dart code to demonstrate how futures and delayed execution work:

```dart
void main() {
  print('Start');

  Future.delayed(Duration(seconds: 1), () {
    print('Future 1');
  });

  Future(() {
    print('Future 2');
  });

  print('End');
}
```

This code snippet demonstrates the order of execution for synchronous code versus asynchronous futures in Dart. Understanding the execution order is critical when building responsive Flutter applications that perform network requests, database queries, or other time-consuming operations.

## Question

What will be the output order when this Dart program is executed?

## Options

A) Start, Future 1, Future 2, End

B) Start, End, Future 2, Future 1

C) Start, End, Future 1, Future 2

D) Future 1, Future 2, Start, End

