# Flutter: Routing

## Scenario

You are developing a Flutter mobile application with multiple screens. The application has two main screens: HomePage and DetailsPage. The HomePage displays a list of items with a button that navigates to the DetailsPage when tapped. The DetailsPage displays detailed information about the selected item.

The following code structure has been started:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(const MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  const MyApp({Key? key}) : super(key: key);

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      title: 'Flutter Routing Demo',
      home: const HomePage(),
    );
  }
}

class HomePage extends StatelessWidget {
  const HomePage({Key? key}) : super(key: key);

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('Home')),
      body: Center(
        child: ElevatedButton(
          onPressed: () {
            __________ // Missing navigation code
          },
          child: const Text('Go to Details'),
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}

class DetailsPage extends StatelessWidget {
  const DetailsPage({Key? key}) : super(key: key);

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('Details')),
      body: const Center(child: Text('This is the Details Page')),
    );
  }
}
```

## Question

Which of the following code snippets should replace the blank line to properly navigate from HomePage to DetailsPage using Flutter's navigation system?

A) `Navigator.of(context).push(Route(builder: (context) => const DetailsPage()));`

B) `Navigator.of(context).push(MaterialPageRoute(builder: (context) => const DetailsPage()));`

C) `context.go('/details');`

D) `Navigator.pushNamed(context, 'details');`

# Flutter: State Management

## Scenario

You are developing a task list app using Flutter. The app displays a list of tasks, each with a checkbox that allows users to mark tasks as complete or incomplete. The task data is stored in a List of Task objects, where each Task has properties like `id`, `title`, and `isCompleted`.

When a user taps a checkbox next to a task, the app should update that task's completion status. The checkbox is connected to an `onChanged` callback that passes the new boolean value.

Here is the relevant code structure:

```dart
class _TaskListState extends State<TaskList> {
  List<Task> tasks = [
    Task(id: 1, title: 'Buy groceries', isCompleted: false),
    Task(id: 2, title: 'Finish project', isCompleted: false),
  ];

  void _toggleTask(int taskId, bool newValue) {
    setState(() {
      // Code to update the task completion status
    });
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return ListView.builder(
      itemCount: tasks.length,
      itemBuilder: (context, index) {
        return CheckboxListTile(
          value: tasks[index].isCompleted,
          onChanged: (newValue) => _toggleTask(tasks[index].id, newValue),
          title: Text(tasks[index].title),
        );
      },
    );
  }
}
```

## Question

Which code snippet correctly completes the `setState` function to update the task's completion status when a checkbox is toggled?

## Options

A) `tasks[taskId].isCompleted = newValue;`

B) `tasks.firstWhere((task) => task.id == taskId).isCompleted = newValue;`

C) `final task = tasks.firstWhere((task) => task.id == taskId); task.isCompleted = newValue;`

D) `tasks.where((task) => task.id == taskId).forEach((task) => task.isCompleted = newValue);`

