# Java Interface

## Scenario

You are reviewing code for a multithreaded Java application. A senior developer has written a method that attempts to create and start multiple threads using a functional interface approach. The code block below represents their implementation:

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Task {
    void execute() throws IOException;
}

public class ThreadManager {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Task task = () -> {
            Thread.sleep(1000);
            System.out.println("Task completed");
        };

        Thread t = new Thread(task);
        t.start();
    }
}
```

The developer is attempting to create a functional interface that defines a task to be executed by a thread. However, when they try to compile this code, they encounter compilation errors. The Task interface declares a checked exception (IOException), and the lambda expression uses Thread.sleep(), which throws InterruptedException (also a checked exception).

## Question

Which of the following best explains why this code will NOT compile, and what is the primary issue with this design?

A) The Task functional interface declares IOException as a checked exception, but Thread.sleep() throws InterruptedException, which is incompatible. The Runnable interface used by Thread does not allow for checked exceptions in its execute method, so the Task interface cannot be properly implemented by a lambda expression that uses Thread.sleep().

B) The @FunctionalInterface annotation is incorrectly placed on an interface that throws checked exceptions. Functional interfaces in Java cannot declare any checked exceptions, regardless of whether they are used with threading or not.

C) The Thread constructor requires a Runnable parameter, which has an execute() method signature that does not throw any checked exceptions. The Task interface's execute() method declares IOException, making Task incompatible with the Runnable contract, and Thread.sleep() throws InterruptedException which is uncaught.

D) The code will compile successfully because the lambda expression properly implements the Task interface. The compilation error will only occur at runtime when Thread.sleep() throws an InterruptedException that is not handled in the main method.

