# Angular: Back-end Communication

## Scenario

An Angular application communicates with the back-end service to manage employee records. One of its features allows authenticated users to add new employees to the system. The application uses JWT tokens stored in localStorage for authentication. The back-end API endpoint is located at `/api/employees` and expects a POST request with employee data in the request body and an Authorization header containing the JWT token.

You are tasked with implementing a service method that:
- Retrieves the JWT token from localStorage
- Sends a POST request with employee data to the back-end
- Includes the Authorization header with the Bearer token
- Returns an Observable of the response

The employee data object contains properties like name, email, department, and salary.

## Question

Which of the following Angular service implementations correctly handles the POST request to add an employee with JWT authentication?

## Options

A) 
```typescript
addEmployee(employee: Employee): Observable<any> {
  const token = localStorage.getItem('token');
  const headers = new HttpHeaders({
    'Authorization': `Bearer ${token}`
  });
  return this.http.post('/api/employees', employee, { headers });
