# Identify Governor Limit Violation in Batch Apex

In Salesforce, governor limits are crucial to ensure efficient use of resources. These limits restrict the number of operations that can be performed within a single transaction or batch execution. Understanding where violations occur is essential for writing scalable Apex code.

Consider the following Batch Apex class designed to update all Account records in the system:

```
global class AccountUpdateBatch implements Database.Batchable<sObject> {
    global Database.QueryLocator start(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        return Database.getQueryLocator('SELECT Id, Name FROM Account');
    }

    global void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List<sObject> scope) {
        List<Account> accountsToUpdate = (List<Account>) scope;
        for(Account acc : accountsToUpdate) {
            acc.Name = acc.Name + ' - Updated';
            for(Integer i = 0; i < 150; i++) {
                Database.query('SELECT Id FROM Contact WHERE AccountId = \'' + acc.Id + '\'');
            }
        }
        update accountsToUpdate;
    }

    global void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        System.debug('Batch process completed');
    }
}
```

Which part of this code is most likely to cause a governor limit violation?

A) The Database.getQueryLocator() method in the start() method, which attempts to query all Account records at once

B) The nested loop inside execute() that performs 150 SOQL queries per Account record within the batch scope

C) The update statement at the end of execute(), which could update too many Account records simultaneously

D) The System.debug() statement in the finish() method, which logs excessive data to the debug log

