# Result of Concurrent Threads

## Scenario

Five threads are accessing a shared resource in a Java application. A common integer variable named `counter` is initialized to 0. Each of the five threads executes the same operation: it increments the `counter` variable 1000 times in a loop. The threads are started simultaneously and run without any synchronization mechanisms (no locks, no synchronized blocks, and no atomic variables). After all five threads complete their execution, the program prints the final value of `counter`.

Consider the following code structure:

```
public class SharedResource {
    public static int counter = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread[] threads = new Thread[5];

        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            threads[i] = new Thread(() -> {
                for (int j = 0; j < 1000; j++) {
                    counter++;
                }
            });
            threads[i].start();
        }

        for (Thread t : threads) {
            t.join();
        }

        System.out.println(counter);
    }
}
```

## Question

What is the most likely final value of `counter` after all five threads have completed execution?

A) 5000

B) Less than 5000

C) Greater than 5000

D) 0
